results.method:
I wrote my own code from scratch in /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-elementFi-takehome/abe89a27-8b28-5869-a9f7-5ac0ed0d524d/scratchpad/verify/numerical/. I did not use the project's solver or topology code, and I modified nothing under /home/user/three-body-problem.

- nb.py: planar Newtonian right-hand side with G=1 and no regularization, plus helpers for the slice initial condition, energy E, angular momentum L and momentum P.
- check.py: integrates each entry with solve_ivp DOP853 at rtol=atol=1e-13 with dense output to the claimed T, and reports:
  - max|Y(T)-Y(0)| over all 12 position and velocity components;
  - the same residual at tolerances 1e-12 and 2.3e-14/1e-15, to show it is integration error;
  - E and T* recomputed from the initial condition and compared with the claim;
  - L(0), max|L(t)| and max|E(t)-E0|;
  - the minimum pairwise distance over one period (grid of 400k dense points plus all solver steps, refined with a bounded minimizer);
  - the minimum of max|Y(t)-Y0| within +-5% of T/n for n=2..6 (refined), and the minimum over the whole interval (0.1T, 0.9T).
- altmeth.py: repeats the closure test with a different integrator family, implicit Radau IIA at rtol=atol=1e-12.
- stab.py: my own 12x12 variational equations give the monodromy matrix and Floquet multipliers (8 trivial multipliers removed). It then runs my own Gauss-Newton on (v1, v2, T) inside the slice to see how far the claimed values sit from the nearest exactly closing solution.
- shaperet.py: looks for returns in shape space (pair distances, their rates, and the angular parts) up to reflection, time reversal and the 1<->2 relabeling.
- parsecat.py: parses the Li & Liao picture tables into JSON (run with python3 -I), so claimed T* and (v1, v2) can be compared with catalogue rows at the same m3.

Outputs are claims_out.json, claims_stab.json, claims.log, claims_stab.log, radau.log, classic.log and classic_stab.json in the same directory.

Each full run took seconds to about 20 s per orbit.
